        {
          "subsection_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "1. Introduction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A breast biopsy needle is a medical instrument used to obtain cells, tissue fragments, or fluid from a breast lesion for pathological examination. It helps distinguish benign breast conditions from malignancy and guides subsequent management."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is commonly used in the investigation of a breast lump, suspicious breast imaging findings, and non-palpable lesions identified by mammography or ultrasound."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important OSCE distinction: The term breast biopsy needle may refer broadly to several needle-based sampling instruments. You must distinguish fine-needle aspiration (FNA), core needle biopsy (CNB), and vacuum-assisted biopsy (VAB)."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/breast_biopsy_needle_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "1. Fine-needle aspiration (FNA)"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Obtains cells or fluid for cytological examination."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Uses a fine-bore needle, often approximately 22–25G.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May be used for cyst aspiration or selected breast lumps.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Does not usually preserve tissue architecture.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/breast_biopsy_needle_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "2. Core needle biopsy (CNB)"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Obtains cylindrical tissue cores for histopathology."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Commonly uses a 14G needle, although other gauges are available.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Usually spring-loaded or automated.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Preserves tissue architecture and permits assessment of tumour type and invasion.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/breast_biopsy_needle_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "3. Vacuum-assisted breast biopsy (VAB)"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Obtains multiple tissue samples through a larger sampling aperture using suction."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Often used for small lesions or mammographic microcalcifications.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May obtain more tissue than conventional core biopsy.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May be used for selected image-guided excisions.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "2. Identification in an OSCE station",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "If an examiner presents a breast biopsy needle, begin with a concise identification statement."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Model answer:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“This is a breast biopsy needle, an instrument used to obtain cellular or tissue samples from a breast lesion for cytological or histopathological examination. Depending on its design, it may be used for fine-needle aspiration, core needle biopsy, or vacuum-assisted biopsy. The procedure may be performed under palpation or image guidance.”",
                  "style": "quote"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Then describe the visible components, type of sampling, indications, advantages, disadvantages and complications."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "3. Labelling and parts",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The precise parts depend on the type of needle presented. For a typical automated core biopsy system, the examiner may ask you to label the following:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/breast_biopsy_needle_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "en.medcanyon.com"
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Part",
                    "Function"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Needle tip",
                      "Penetrates tissue and guides the needle into the target."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Stylet or inner needle",
                      "Contains the tissue sampling notch in many core biopsy systems."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Sampling notch",
                      "Receives the tissue core before it is cut."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Cutting cannula",
                      "Advances over the notch to sever the tissue specimen."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Needle shaft",
                      "Provides access to the target lesion."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Handle or trigger",
                      "Controls the spring-loaded sampling mechanism."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Depth stop, if present",
                      "Helps limit insertion depth."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Coaxial introducer, if used",
                      "Creates an access tract through which the biopsy needle can be passed."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Vacuum connection, if present",
                      "Applies suction to draw tissue into the sampling aperture."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Exam tip: Do not label every device as having a trigger or sampling notch. A simple FNA needle has neither a spring-loaded mechanism nor a core sampling notch."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. Properties and characteristics",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A breast biopsy needle is designed to obtain diagnostic material while minimising trauma to surrounding breast tissue."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Its properties include:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Material: Usually medical-grade stainless steel or another suitable biocompatible material.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Sterility: Commonly supplied sterile and intended for single use.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Sharpness: A sharp tip facilitates controlled penetration.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Gauge: Determines the approximate needle diameter. A lower gauge number indicates a larger diameter.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Length: Varies according to lesion depth, approach and device design.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Sampling mechanism: Aspiration, cutting, automated core sampling or vacuum assistance.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Image compatibility: Selected devices can be used under ultrasound, stereotactic mammographic or MRI guidance, according to manufacturer specifications.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. Classification and types",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Breast biopsy needles can be classified by their sampling mechanism."
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Type",
                    "Specimen obtained",
                    "Typical application"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Fine-needle aspiration",
                      "Cells or fluid",
                      "Cyst aspiration or selected palpable lesions"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Manual core needle",
                      "Tissue cores",
                      "Histological diagnosis of a breast mass"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Automated core biopsy gun",
                      "Tissue cores",
                      "Image-guided sampling of suspicious lesions"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Vacuum-assisted biopsy needle",
                      "Multiple tissue samples",
                      "Microcalcifications, selected small lesions and repeat sampling"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Large-core biopsy device",
                      "Larger tissue cores",
                      "Selected lesions requiring larger samples"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "They may also be classified by guidance method:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Palpation-guided.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ultrasound-guided.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Stereotactic or tomosynthesis-guided.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "MRI-guided.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The method chosen depends on how the lesion is visualised and the expertise and equipment available."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. Principle of operation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The principle is to obtain representative diagnostic material from a suspicious breast lesion through a minimally invasive approach."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Core biopsy principle:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "The lesion is localised by palpation or imaging.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The needle is advanced into the target under appropriate guidance.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The sampling mechanism captures tissue.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The cutting cannula severs the specimen in a core biopsy system.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The sample is removed, placed in the appropriate specimen container and sent for histopathological examination.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "For FNA, negative pressure and needle movement collect cells or fluid instead of an intact tissue core."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Indications and uses",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Breast biopsy may be indicated for:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "A suspicious palpable breast lump.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A lesion classified as suspicious on mammography or ultrasound.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Suspicious microcalcifications.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A non-palpable breast lesion requiring image-guided sampling.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Suspected breast cancer before definitive treatment.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Histological assessment of a suspected recurrent lesion.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Investigation of suspicious axillary lymph nodes using an appropriate sampling device.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Evaluation of a lesion when imaging and clinical findings require tissue diagnosis.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A breast cyst may require aspiration rather than core biopsy, depending on its clinical and imaging features."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Surgeries and procedures in which it is used",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The biopsy needle is primarily a diagnostic instrument rather than a conventional surgical cutting instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "It may be used during:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Ultrasound-guided breast core needle biopsy.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Stereotactic breast biopsy.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "MRI-guided breast biopsy.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Image-guided biopsy of selected axillary lymph nodes.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Vacuum-assisted breast biopsy or selected percutaneous lesion removal.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Preoperative diagnosis before breast-conserving surgery or mastectomy.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important: A biopsy needle does not itself perform a mastectomy or lumpectomy. It provides a tissue diagnosis that may guide the decision to perform these operations."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Minimally invasive: Requires a small skin puncture rather than a large incision.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Usually outpatient: Many procedures can be completed without hospital admission.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Local anaesthesia: Often sufficient for percutaneous biopsy.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Rapid recovery: Most patients resume normal activities relatively quickly.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Diagnostic value: Core biopsy provides tissue architecture for histological assessment.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Image guidance: Allows accurate sampling of lesions that cannot be felt clinically.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Treatment planning: Tissue can be assessed for tumour characteristics and, when indicated, biomarkers such as oestrogen receptor, progesterone receptor and HER2.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Sampling error may produce a false-negative or non-diagnostic result.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Small lesions or difficult locations may be challenging to sample.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "FNA may not distinguish in-situ from invasive disease because it does not provide tissue architecture.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Core biopsy may yield insufficient tissue or miss the target.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Some patients experience pain, bruising or anxiety.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A suspicious result may require additional biopsy or surgical excision.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Imaging and pathology results may be discordant, necessitating further investigation.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "11. Complications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Bleeding and haematoma"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The most important common procedural complications. Risk may be increased by anticoagulants, bleeding disorders or larger sampling devices."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Pain and bruising"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Usually mild and temporary, although discomfort may persist."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Infection"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uncommon when appropriate aseptic technique is used."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Rare complications"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "These include vasovagal reactions and, depending on lesion location and technique, injury to adjacent structures such as the chest wall."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_group_13",
              "group_title": "Prevention of complications",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Confirm patient identity, lesion site and consent.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Review bleeding risks and medications.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Use appropriate sterile technique and local anaesthesia.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Use imaging guidance when indicated.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Apply pressure after sampling and assess haemostasis.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Provide aftercare instructions and clear advice on when to seek medical attention.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Anticoagulants should not be stopped automatically; decisions must be based on the medication, bleeding risk and local protocol."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "12. Contraindications and precautions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "There are few absolute contraindications to percutaneous breast biopsy, but precautions include:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Significant uncorrected bleeding tendency.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Anticoagulant or antiplatelet treatment requiring individual assessment.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Local infection at the intended puncture site.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Inability to cooperate or remain appropriately positioned.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A lesion location that makes safe needle access difficult.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Pregnancy is not, by itself, a contraindication to breast biopsy. The imaging modality and procedural plan should be selected appropriately."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "13. Specimen handling",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This is a frequently overlooked OSCE area."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Confirm that the specimen corresponds to the correct patient and lesion.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Label the container with the patient's identifiers and specimen site.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Place tissue cores in the appropriate fixative, commonly neutral buffered formalin, according to the receiving laboratory's protocol.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Send the specimen with a clinical history and relevant imaging information.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "If microbiological studies or specialised testing are required, follow the laboratory's specific collection instructions.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ensure that the radiological and pathological findings are reviewed together for concordance.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key distinction: Cytology specimens from FNA and tissue cores from CNB are processed differently. Do not automatically place every aspirate in formalin."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_subsection_15",
          "subsection_title": "14. Interpretation and limitations of results",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_group_16",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The biopsy result should be interpreted alongside the clinical examination and imaging findings."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Benign and concordant: The pathology explains the imaging appearance; follow-up is planned according to the lesion and local protocol.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Malignant: Histological diagnosis and relevant tumour biomarkers guide treatment planning.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Inadequate or non-diagnostic: The sample is insufficient for a reliable conclusion; repeat sampling or another diagnostic procedure may be necessary.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Benign but discordant: A benign result does not settle the case if imaging or clinical findings remain suspicious. Repeat biopsy or surgical excision may be required.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A benign biopsy result should never automatically override strongly suspicious imaging or clinical findings."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_subsection_16",
          "subsection_title": "15. High-yield OSCE questions and answers",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_group_17",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is the main purpose of a breast biopsy needle?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "To obtain cells, fluid or tissue from a breast lesion for cytological or histopathological diagnosis."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is the difference between FNA and core needle biopsy?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "FNA obtains cells or fluid for cytology; core needle biopsy obtains tissue cores that preserve architecture and allow histological assessment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What is a commonly used gauge for core breast biopsy?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A 14G needle is common, although gauge varies with the device and clinical indication."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. Which imaging modalities guide breast biopsy?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Ultrasound, stereotactic mammography or tomosynthesis, and MRI, depending on how the lesion is best visualised."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What is the most important common complication?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Bleeding or haematoma formation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. What does a benign biopsy with suspicious imaging findings mean?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Possible radiology–pathology discordance. Further assessment, repeat biopsy or surgical excision may be necessary."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Why is core biopsy often preferred when breast cancer is suspected?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It provides tissue architecture and allows histological classification and biomarker testing, which are important for treatment planning."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. Does a biopsy needle remove the entire breast tumour?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Usually not. Its main purpose is diagnostic sampling. Vacuum-assisted devices can remove selected small lesions, but this is not equivalent to definitive oncological surgery."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_subsection_17",
          "subsection_title": "16. Examiner's checklist",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "breast_biopsy_needle_group_18",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
